\title{A Second Law of Thermodynamics for Mathematical Proof}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a quantitative thermodynamics of logical derivation. Modelling an
inference step as a function $f$ between finite state registers, we define the
\emph{information erased} by the step as the drop in the base-two logarithm of
the number of distinguishable register states, and its \emph{Landauer cost} as
$k_B T \ln 2$ joules per erased bit. We prove: erasure is nonnegative; a step
erases zero bits if and only if it is injective (logically reversible); any
irreversible step dissipates strictly positive heat at positive temperature;
erasure obeys a data-processing inequality; and Bennett's input-retaining
dilation erases nothing. We then lift the theory to entire derivations, modelled
as finite sequences of steps on a fixed register. Charging each step its
\emph{marginal} entropy production, we establish a \emph{discrete Clausius
inequality}: total erased information equals a sum of nonnegative per-step
productions. From it we derive a \emph{second law}---dissipated heat is monotone
under extension---and a reversibility criterion: a derivation dissipates zero
heat iff its composite is injective, which holds exactly when every step is
reversible. A \emph{creation/erasure ledger} makes Bennett's trade-off precise:
his dilation of $f:\alpha\to\beta$ erases nothing but creates exactly
$\log_2\card{\beta}$ bits of ancilla. Finally we exhibit families whose erasure
grows linearly and exponentially, and a counting bound guaranteeing predicates
whose verification erases at least $n$ bits.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Landauer's principle asserts that erasing one bit of information at temperature
$T$ dissipates at least $k_B T \ln 2$ joules of heat. It bridges information
theory and thermodynamics: logically irreversible operations have an unavoidable
physical cost, while reversible ones may in principle be free. Bennett sharpened
the picture by embedding any computation in a reversible one, concentrating the
thermodynamic cost in the final act of discarding information.

This paper applies the Landauer--Bennett viewpoint to \emph{proof}. A formal
derivation is a sequence of inference steps, each transforming the ambient
logical state. Viewing that state as a finite register and each step as a
function on registers, we ask how much information a derivation erases and hence
how much heat it must dissipate. We build a two-tier theory: a single-step
calculus of erasure (Sections~\ref{sec:single}--\ref{sec:adjudicate}), and a
\emph{pipeline} theory in which an entire derivation obeys a discrete second law
(Sections~\ref{sec:pipeline}--\ref{sec:reversible}). Section~\ref{sec:large}
quantifies growth of the cost, and Section~\ref{sec:ledger} records the
creation/erasure ledger.

\section{Preliminaries}
A \emph{register} is a nonempty finite type $\alpha$ with cardinality
$\card{\alpha}\ge 1$. For $f:\alpha\to\beta$, $\imcard(f)=\card{\operatorname{image}f}$
is the number of distinct values it attains. We use repeatedly:
\begin{itemize}
  \item[(P1)] $1 \le \imcard(f) \le \card{\alpha}$ on a nonempty register;
  \item[(P2)] $\imcard(g\circ f) \le \imcard(f)$: post-composition cannot enlarge
  an image.
\end{itemize}
All logarithms are base two, and $\log_2$ is strictly increasing on $(0,\infty)$.

\section{Erasure and Landauer cost of a single step}\label{sec:single}

\begin{definition}[Erased information]
For $f:\alpha\to\beta$ between finite registers,
$\erased(f) = \log_2\card{\alpha} - \log_2\imcard(f)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Landauer cost]
For $b$ erased bits, $\landauer(b,k_B,T) = b\cdot k_B\cdot T\cdot\ln 2$; thus
$\landauer(1,k_B,T)=k_B T\ln 2$ is the classical Landauer quantum.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Nonnegativity]
$\erased(f)\ge 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By (P1), $\imcard(f)\le\card{\alpha}$; monotonicity of $\log_2$ gives
$\log_2\imcard(f)\le\log_2\card{\alpha}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reversibility criterion]\label{thm:rev}
$\erased(f)=0$ iff $f$ is injective.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\erased(f)=0$ iff $\imcard(f)=\card{\alpha}$ (injectivity of $\log_2$), which for
a function on a finite set holds precisely when $f$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Landauer's principle]
If $f$ is not injective and $k_B,T>0$, then $\landauer(\erased(f),k_B,T)>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Non-injectivity gives $\erased(f)>0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:rev}; multiply by the
positive constant $k_B T\ln 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Register lower bound]
If $f:\alpha\to\beta$ then $\erased(f)\ge\log_2\card{\alpha}-\log_2\card{\beta}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\imcard(f)\le\card{\beta}$ since the image lies in the codomain; apply
monotonicity of $\log_2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Data-processing inequality]\label{thm:dpi}
For $f:\alpha\to\beta$ and $g:\beta\to\gamma$, post-composition never decreases
erasure relative to the original domain: $\erased(f)\le\erased(g\circ f)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By (P2), $\imcard(g\circ f)\le\imcard(f)$; subtract the corresponding logarithms
from the common $\log_2\card{\alpha}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bennett dilation is free]\label{thm:bennettfree}
For any $f:\alpha\to\beta$, the map $B_f(x)=(x,f(x))$ satisfies $\erased(B_f)=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$B_f$ is injective (its first coordinate is the identity), so
Theorem~\ref{thm:rev} applies.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Adjudicating four conjectures}\label{sec:adjudicate}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{False:} ``every non-identity step erases.'' The NOT gate is a
  nontrivial bijection with $\erased=0$.
  \item \textbf{True:} the two-bit AND gate erases exactly
  $\log_2 4-\log_2 2=1$ bit.
  \item \textbf{False:} additivity $\erased(g\circ f)=\erased(f)+\erased(g)$; the
  correct law is sub-additivity (Theorem~\ref{thm:dpi}).
  \item \textbf{True:} every bijection is free.
\end{itemize}
The failure of additivity motivates charging each step its \emph{marginal}
production.

\section{Derivations as pipelines}\label{sec:pipeline}

\begin{definition}[Pipeline and composite]
Fix a register $\alpha$. A \emph{derivation} is a list $\mathrm{fs}=[f_1,\dots,f_k]$
with $f_i:\alpha\to\alpha$, with composite
$\compose(\mathrm{fs})=f_k\circ\cdots\circ f_1$ and $\compose([])=\mathrm{id}$.
Appending post-composes: $\compose(\mathrm{fs}\cat[g])=g\circ\compose(\mathrm{fs})$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Total erased information]
$\totalErased(\mathrm{fs})=\erased(\compose(\mathrm{fs}))$. Immediately
$\totalErased([])=0$ and $\totalErased(\mathrm{fs})\ge 0$.
\end{definition}

\section{Per-step entropy production and the ledger identity}

\begin{definition}[Step entropy production]
$\stepDrop(\mathrm{fs},g)=\log_2\imcard(\compose(\mathrm{fs}))-\log_2\imcard(\compose(\mathrm{fs}\cat[g]))$,
the reduction in distinguishable states caused by $g$ in context.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Per-step data processing]\label{thm:stepnn}
$\stepDrop(\mathrm{fs},g)\ge 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
With $F=\compose(\mathrm{fs})$, the new composite is $g\circ F$ and (P2) gives
$\imcard(g\circ F)\le\imcard(F)$; monotonicity of $\log_2$ finishes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Ledger identity]\label{thm:ledger}
$\totalErased(\mathrm{fs}\cat[g])=\totalErased(\mathrm{fs})+\stepDrop(\mathrm{fs},g)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both totals share the domain term $\log_2\card{\alpha}$; subtracting, it cancels
and the remainder is exactly $\stepDrop(\mathrm{fs},g)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The second law and the discrete Clausius inequality}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity---second law]\label{thm:mono}
$\totalErased(\mathrm{fs})\le\totalErased(\mathrm{fs}\cat\mathrm{gs})$ for any
suffix $\mathrm{gs}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $\mathrm{gs}$ from the right using associativity of concatenation, the
ledger identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:ledger}), and $\stepDrop\ge0$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:stepnn}).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Discrete Clausius inequality]\label{thm:clausius}
For $\mathrm{fs}=[f_1,\dots,f_k]$ there exist $d_1,\dots,d_k\ge 0$, one per
inference, with $\sum_{i=1}^k d_i=\totalErased(\mathrm{fs})$ and
$d_i=\stepDrop([f_1,\dots,f_{i-1}],f_i)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct from the right: the empty pipeline gives the empty sum $0$; appending $g$
appends the nonnegative term $\stepDrop(\mathrm{fs},g)$ and the sum updates by the
ledger identity to $\totalErased(\mathrm{fs}\cat[g])$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Heat monotonicity]
Let $\totalHeat(\mathrm{fs},k_B,T)=\landauer(\totalErased(\mathrm{fs}),k_B,T)$. If
$k_B,T\ge 0$ then $\totalHeat(\mathrm{fs},k_B,T)\le\totalHeat(\mathrm{fs}\cat\mathrm{gs},k_B,T)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Multiply Theorem~\ref{thm:mono} by the nonnegative constant $k_B T\ln 2$.
\end{proof}

\section{Reversibility of derivations}\label{sec:reversible}

\begin{theorem}[Reversibility criterion for pipelines]\label{thm:revpipe}
$\totalErased(\mathrm{fs})=0$ iff $\compose(\mathrm{fs})$ is injective.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:rev} to the composite.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reversible derivations are free]
If every step of $\mathrm{fs}$ is injective, then $\totalErased(\mathrm{fs})=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A composite of injective maps is injective (induction on the list); apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:revpipe}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
By the Clausius decomposition, the total vanishes exactly when each
$\stepDrop$ vanishes, i.e. when each prefix composite is injective. Reversibility
is thus a local property, certifiable by a left-to-right scan of consecutive
image sizes without forming the whole composite.
\end{remark}

\section{How large can the cost be?}\label{sec:large}

\textbf{Linear family.} A step $\operatorname{collapse}_n$ on an $n$-bit register
with single-point image has $\erased(\operatorname{collapse}_n)=\log_2 2^n-\log_2 1=n$.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{Exponential family.} A step
$\operatorname{bigCollapse}_m$ collapsing $2^m$ states has
$\erased(\operatorname{bigCollapse}_m)=2^m$, so the erasure grows exponentially and
the separation from the linear family is unbounded.

\medskip\noindent\textbf{Kolmogorov counting bound.} There are $2^n$ Boolean
predicates on $n$ input bits but only $\sum_{\ell<n}2^\ell=2^n-1$ programs of
length $<n$. By pigeonhole some predicate $P$ is incompressible; verifying it
requires resolving its $2^n$-entry truth table, erasing at least $n$ bits and
dissipating at least $n\cdot k_B T\ln 2$ joules.

\section{The creation/erasure ledger}\label{sec:ledger}

\begin{definition}[Created bits]
Growing an $a$-state register to a $b$-state register creates
$\created(a,b)=\log_2 b-\log_2 a$ bits; $\created(a,b)\ge0$ when $1\le a\le b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Bennett trade-off]
For $f:\alpha\to\beta$, the dilation $B_f(x)=(x,f(x))$ satisfies $\erased(B_f)=0$
and $\created(\card{\alpha},\card{\alpha\times\beta})=\log_2\card{\beta}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Erasure vanishes by Theorem~\ref{thm:bennettfree}. For creation,
$\card{\alpha\times\beta}=\card{\alpha}\card{\beta}$, so
$\created=\log_2(\card{\alpha}\card{\beta})-\log_2\card{\alpha}=\log_2\card{\beta}$
by the product rule.
\end{proof}

Erasure and creation are two columns of one ledger: logical irreversibility can
always be traded for allocation, at cost exactly $\log_2\card{\beta}$ bits.

\section{Applications and discussion}
The classical dichotomy between elegant and brute-force proofs acquires a
physical reading: elegant derivations are reversible (zero dissipation),
brute-force ones forget case analysis and pay Landauer's toll per bit; the second
law formalizes that padding a proof never makes it cheaper. The incompressibility
bound ties verification heat to description length, suggesting a thermodynamic
route to proof-complexity lower bounds. The creation/erasure ledger indicates a
design principle for low-dissipation reasoning: retain intermediate data (paying
in memory) rather than discard it (paying in heat). The model measures logical
irreversibility of idealized register maps and gives a lower bound on physical
dissipation, not a device model.

\section{Future directions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A Clausius inequality for branching derivations (proof DAGs): total
  dissipation as a sum of nonnegative local productions over edges, invariant
  under topological re-serialization.
  \item Reversibility as a local, linear-time checkable property: zero
  dissipation iff every prefix map is injective.
  \item Tightness of the creation/erasure trade-off: Bennett's embedding
  minimizes created ancilla, with minimum $\log_2\card{\beta}$.
  \item Kolmogorov complexity proper: verifying $x$ from a shortest certificate
  erases $\ge K(x)-O(1)$ bits.
  \item Exponential proof-vs-answer gaps and per-rule erasure costs (resolution,
  modus ponens, cut) to compare calculi.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
Mathematical derivation admits a genuine thermodynamics. Single steps have an
exact erasure and Landauer cost, with reversibility characterized by injectivity.
Entire derivations obey a discrete Clausius inequality: total dissipation is a
sum of nonnegative per-step productions, monotone under extension, and zero
exactly for reversible derivations. Bennett's trade-off, made precise as a
creation/erasure ledger, shows reversibility is financed by allocation rather
than granted for free, and explicit families show the cost is unbounded and
sometimes intrinsic. Information is physical; so, we conclude, is proof.

